1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,840
End of story.

2
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:03,160
All right, and you say, what's the problem with that?

3
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:05,240
Well, there might be some cost

4
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,160
of fixing that Nedley-Loss Triangle.

5
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:09,120
Like at minimum, there's gonna be the cost

6
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:10,320
of paying the bureaucrats

7
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:12,880
and having their bureau building, right?

8
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:13,720
If you walk around D.C.,

9
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,080
there's all these bureau buildings.

10
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:17,840
Those resources have to come from somewhere.

11
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,020
So suppose that the cost of fixing

12
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:24,360
that Nedley-Loss Triangle was that much

13
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:26,280
in bureaucrats and buildings.

14
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:27,860
It would not make sense to go

15
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:30,600
and have government go and fix

16
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,800
and spend that much in resources

17
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,280
to fix that much in losses, right?

18
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,340
That would be, technical term, stupid

19
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:40,880
because you are, if the loss is relatively small,

20
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:43,040
the cost of fixing it is much higher.

21
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:44,700
It is rather more economically efficient

22
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,000
to live with the problem, right?

23
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,680
And could people really be so dumb?

24
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:52,400
Well, I had a lot of undergraduate courses

25
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,280
where professors who knew the real world

26
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:57,080
would go and put these diagrams on there

27
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:59,360
and then say, and what is the solution?

28
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:01,000
Not what could be the solution,

29
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,740
what might be the solution if the cost is low,

30
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:03,940
but what is the solution

31
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:06,640
and the solution is government intervention.

32
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,840
And very often the specifics,

33
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:10,400
the details of the intervention

34
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,400
were so wildly impractical,

35
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,280
you could never do it in the real world,

36
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:15,440
as we shall see.

37
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:16,700
And then you're really wondering,

38
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:18,880
so there's a problem here

39
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:21,800
and your proposed solution is completely undoable

40
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,240
and that's an argument for government, how?

41
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:27,200
Just like, wait, is this an argument for government

42
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:30,000
in a homework problem or in the real world?

43
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,240
And the tone was always, well, of course, in the real world,

44
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:35,840
but the answer only applied in a homework problem.

45
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:39,680
So, shortcomings of government according to Demsets

46
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:41,320
and I think this is a very fair description

47
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:43,800
of the welfare economics of his era, right?

48
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:46,480
And even basic overhead almost never factored in.

49
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:50,680
So, classic example of the intervention policy.

50
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,680
We go and we take a look at something like this,

51
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:54,480
we have a monopoly and we see the price

52
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,280
is greater than marginal cost.

53
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,160
Right, in fact, we have,

54
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:00,800
probably this is where marginal revenue

55
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:02,200
equals marginal cost, whatever that's.

56
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:03,480
And so you've all seen

57
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:05,500
deadweight loss of monopoly at some point.

58
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:07,440
All right, so we're right here,

59
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,200
leading to this deadweight loss.

60
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,120
All right, now, the textbook solution

61
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:14,280
is just to impose a price control

62
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:16,740
set requiring price to be equal to marginal cost.

63
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:19,920
Right, and wait, how come your price controls don't work?

64
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:21,720
Well, if there's a monopoly

65
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:24,800
set up exactly like this, price controls work great.

66
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:27,360
So, if the world is exactly like this,

67
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,120
then all we do is say, well,

68
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:31,040
price must be equal to that.

69
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:32,680
And if price must be equal to that,

70
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:36,040
then the monopolist maximizes profits

71
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:38,480
just by going and producing equal to marginal cost

72
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,280
and problem solved.

73
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:42,800
Okay, now this immediately gives rise

74
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:44,340
in the real world to a secondary problem,

75
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:48,480
which is that all real world monopolies have fixed costs.

76
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:51,240
So, if you make firms charge a price equal to marginal cost

77
00:02:51,240 --> 00:02:54,560
and there are fixed costs, then all firms lose money.

78
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:57,520
And therefore, the regulations effect

79
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:59,520
will not be to reduce the price of the product,

80
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:02,280
but rather to destroy the existence of the product,

81
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:04,160
which is not the intention, right?

82
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:05,280
So, the goal of the price control

83
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:07,040
was not to eliminate the product from existence,

84
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:09,880
it was to eliminate that deadweight loss.

85
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:11,880
All right, now, then of course,

86
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:12,960
there's a standard solution to that.

87
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:15,440
Well, we'll just go and subsidize firms

88
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:16,760
at amount equal to their fixed cost

89
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:18,680
and that will keep them in business.

90
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:19,880
So, we have the price control

91
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:21,600
and then we have the subsidy to cover the fixed costs

92
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,560
and now everything's great, right?

93
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:25,320
Well, then there is a tertiary problem.

94
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:27,840
How are we gonna fund these subsidies?

95
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:30,480
With lump sum taxes.

96
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,660
All right, and which are the countries on earth

97
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:35,320
that have lump sum taxes again?

98
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:37,720
Which are, where are they?

99
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:38,640
Which ones are these?

100
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:42,060
There are none, as far as I know, zero countries like this.

101
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:43,360
So, in the real world,

102
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:46,660
well, like what kind of taxes would pay for the subsidies?

103
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:48,520
So, for almost all countries,

104
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:51,360
these would be your usual disturbance of taxes.

105
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:52,920
And remember, what is the essence

106
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:54,400
of what a tax assertion does?

107
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:55,600
No, what a tax assertion,

108
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,640
what is the essence of what a tax assertion does?

109
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:01,880
It is to raise price above marginal cost.

110
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:05,800
It is to create a wedge between the value that is received

111
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:07,720
by the person delivering the product

112
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:10,300
and the amount they are paid.

113
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:14,760
So, you produce more value than you actually receive,

114
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:16,700
which then leads again to this deadweight cost.

115
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:19,560
In fact, if you estimate the deadweight cost of taxation,

116
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:21,440
you also get a triangle.

117
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:24,500
It is exactly the same geometrically.

118
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,640
All right, so then what about the deadweight cost of taxes?

119
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:29,640
What about those?

120
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:32,280
All right, and then notice I haven't even covered

121
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:33,680
some of the other obvious problems,

122
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:36,400
like what is marginal cost, what are fixed costs,

123
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:38,640
how would you discover what they are?

124
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:39,760
Are you just gonna ask people?

125
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:41,440
If you do, what are your fixed costs, man?

126
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,640
Because I'm giving you a subsidy, which equals something.

127
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:47,600
You could do that that's not a very effective method.

128
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:50,320
At least that won't drive firms out of business.

129
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:52,720
They'll all stay in business once you do that.

130
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,340
Maybe they might be killed by the taxes

131
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:56,680
that have to be paid in order to pay

132
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,400
for the subsidies for everybody else.

133
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:02,200
But still, but then you might say,

134
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:03,840
no, let's go and have a panel to figure out

135
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:05,360
what your fixed costs are.

136
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:08,360
And so that creates problems as well.

137
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:10,280
So maybe, so if the number's too high,

138
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:13,400
then you are going and imposing unnecessary deadweight costs

139
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:14,760
for excessive taxation.

140
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:16,360
If it's lower, then again, you just wind up

141
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:20,040
destroying the firms that you are regulating.

142
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:21,720
Right, and then strategically, of course,

143
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:23,720
if you run one of these firms,

144
00:05:23,720 --> 00:05:26,280
you might want to pretend to die in order to say,

145
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:29,280
oh, I'm about to die if only my subsidy

146
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:31,280
would not have to cover my fixed costs.

147
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,440
Then I could continue supplying food to the children.

148
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:36,480
But you can't do that because the subsidies

149
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:37,680
won't remain sufficient.

150
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:41,200
All right, and then you're having to fire all of my employees

151
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:43,440
except for me now.

152
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:46,600
And the subsidy, you can fight in your heart

153
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:48,200
to go and double the subsidy,

154
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:49,680
and maybe I could come back from this.

155
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:50,920
So you could try that.

156
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:54,480
So why does Dems test his alternative

157
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:56,280
to the crummy welfare economics

158
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:59,400
that almost everyone by himself was doing at the time?

159
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:02,000
Well, what he said is that economists should use

160
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,520
what he called a comparative institutions approach,

161
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:06,320
which begins with the motto,

162
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:08,720
nothing is perfectly efficient.

163
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:12,120
We never are going to be in a perfectly counter,

164
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:14,400
in a perfectly counter exhibition world.

165
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:16,120
So instead, we should be asking

166
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:18,200
what out of all the feasible options,

167
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:21,520
which one gives us the lowest deadweight costs?

168
00:06:21,520 --> 00:06:23,720
Which one gives, which one leaves,

169
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:26,760
makes us closest to the efficient outcome?

170
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:31,760
Right, and associated with this approach is a,

171
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:32,760
what is it called?

172
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:35,000
A, where's the word for it again?

173
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:37,480
An allegory, that's an allegory.

174
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:40,760
So an allegory is a story where each of the characters

175
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:44,760
and events stands for some other characters and events.

176
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:47,520
So you know George Orwell's Animal Farm, it's an allegory.

177
00:06:47,520 --> 00:06:49,480
Napoleon is Joseph Stalin.

178
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:51,440
Snowball is Leon Trotsky.

179
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:54,400
The Raven is Rasputin, supposedly, and so on.

180
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:56,520
All right, so, and like the windmill getting blown up

181
00:06:56,520 --> 00:06:58,240
the second time is World War II.

182
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:00,720
Right, so if you go and have your cliff notes

183
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:01,560
and you read through it,

184
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:03,280
you will map this correspondence

185
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:04,640
between the characters and events

186
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:07,840
and other real world characters and events.

187
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:11,480
So anyway, the Tale of the Emperor is an allegory

188
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:12,800
and it goes like this.

189
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:15,840
The Emperor of Rome announces a great musical contest

190
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:18,360
to find the best musician in the entire empire.

191
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:21,360
Only two musicians show up.

192
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:24,000
The first musician begins to play, but he misses a note.

193
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,020
So the Emperor says the contest is over.

194
00:07:27,020 --> 00:07:29,160
The second musician is the greatest in the realm

195
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:30,520
and he has the prize.

196
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:31,360
The end.

197
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:33,880
That's the allegory.

198
00:07:33,880 --> 00:07:36,040
All right, so how is it an allegory?

199
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:37,880
Well, the Roman Emperor is supposed to be

200
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:39,880
the mainstream economics profession.

201
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,680
The first musician who misses one note

202
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:43,120
is supposed to be the free market.

203
00:07:43,120 --> 00:07:45,840
And the second musician that we haven't even bothered

204
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:47,320
looking at and listening to how he plays

205
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:49,400
is supposed to be government.

206
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:52,440
And this allegory is a criticism of welfare economics

207
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:57,080
of the 50s and 60s and 70s for economists

208
00:07:57,080 --> 00:07:59,280
going and finding any fault they can in free markets

209
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:01,720
and then saying that government can, will, should,

210
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:03,640
and must fix it without ever bothering

211
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:05,640
to actually investigate how government really works

212
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:06,560
in the real world.

213
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:08,400
And of course, there's a very strong public choice theme

214
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:09,920
here of saying, why don't we look at how government

215
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:11,880
actually functions?

216
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:14,200
I remember when I was the TA

217
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:16,140
for Industrial Organization of Princeton,

218
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:18,560
the professor was, young assistant professor,

219
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:20,480
only knew models, only knew theory,

220
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:23,720
knew no empirics, knew no history of any kind.

221
00:08:23,720 --> 00:08:26,560
So when he was teaching antitrust,

222
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,700
I would occasionally, anytime possible,

223
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:31,120
bring up actual antitrust cases that were relevant,

224
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:33,120
pointing out how the actual antitrust cases

225
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:36,680
had been often did the opposite of what he said

226
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:38,080
would be a good idea.

227
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:39,360
And finally said, you know, Brian,

228
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:41,760
I don't care about any of the history of antitrust.

229
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:44,840
All that matters is what policy,

230
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:46,880
all that matters is just giving the best policy advice

231
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:50,000
to the government so it can actually do a good job.

232
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,080
And I said, well, but shouldn't we look

233
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:54,760
at the past history of antitrust to get an idea

234
00:08:54,760 --> 00:08:56,760
about what government is actually likely to do

235
00:08:56,760 --> 00:08:58,200
and how the extent of government

236
00:08:58,200 --> 00:09:00,480
is actually likely to listen to good advice?

237
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:01,920
And then he said, well, by that standard,

238
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:03,480
we would never have any governments.

239
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:07,520
And I said, that's way stronger than I would have said.

240
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:10,200
But anyway, it's a good concession.

241
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:11,880
Yes.

242
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:12,700
Who was the professor?

243
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:13,800
I'm not gonna name names.

244
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:17,280
He eventually did some empirics and got tenure.

245
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:19,400
But not a great story.

246
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:23,160
All right, so that's the tale of the emperor.

247
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:25,280
Now when you add more constraints to a standard problem,

248
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:27,760
the original optimum is no longer feasible.

249
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:29,160
And it's very common for economists

250
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:32,000
to call the original optimum with very few constraints,

251
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:33,560
the first best optimum,

252
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:35,440
and the optimum with the additional constraints

253
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:37,560
as a second best optimum.

254
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:39,280
So it's common, for example,

255
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:41,080
to say that setting price equal to marginal cost

256
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:42,560
is the first best optimum,

257
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:45,480
setting price equal to average cost is the second best.

258
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:48,380
So markets will often give you average cost pricing,

259
00:09:48,380 --> 00:09:51,520
but very rarely will they give you marginal cost pricing.

260
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:53,880
And intuitively, think about the route

261
00:09:53,880 --> 00:09:55,240
that you drove to school today.

262
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:56,360
Now imagine what your route would be

263
00:09:56,360 --> 00:10:00,000
if you had to stop by Kentucky Fried Chicken on the way.

264
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,260
It is very likely that that would be a different route

265
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:04,600
when you add on that extra constraint.

266
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:05,960
Unless by sheer coincidence,

267
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:07,360
you already are driving right past

268
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:08,520
Kentucky Fried Chicken.

269
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:11,360
In general, any time you add additional constraints

270
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:12,880
to an optimization problem,

271
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:15,160
it changes the result and gives you something

272
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:19,760
that is harder to do because you added on more constraints.

273
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:22,360
And that is the idea of why adding on more constraints

274
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:24,140
goes and changes the results.

275
00:10:24,140 --> 00:10:25,240
So sometimes people talk about,

276
00:10:25,240 --> 00:10:28,080
what if you add in public choice constraints, for example?

277
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:29,720
How does that change the optimum?

278
00:10:29,720 --> 00:10:31,400
And every now and then, economists will talk about

279
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:33,760
a policy being 15th best.

280
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:36,480
This is where it's so weighed down with the baggage,

281
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:38,600
the real world, it looks horrifying.

282
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:41,280
But where you say, look, given the fact that

283
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:43,280
we couldn't get anything unless

284
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:46,600
congressmen from Colorado got a bow and arrow subsidy,

285
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:50,840
and unless Haiti got a new water slide,

286
00:10:50,840 --> 00:10:53,160
and unless we declared war on one country,

287
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:54,520
didn't matter which one,

288
00:10:54,520 --> 00:10:57,320
and unless we did all these things,

289
00:10:57,320 --> 00:10:58,800
that's the only way to get to this deal,

290
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:00,660
so it's 15th best.

291
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:01,500
Sorry.

292
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:03,360
People will sometimes say that.

293
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:05,320
All right.

294
00:11:05,320 --> 00:11:08,080
So, questions, questions?

295
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:08,920
All right.

296
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:11,160
So, now, big question.

297
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:12,240
Who cares about efficiency anyway?

298
00:11:12,240 --> 00:11:15,380
We spent now two hours talking about efficiency,

299
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:17,240
and many people say, well,

300
00:11:17,240 --> 00:11:18,880
what difference does it make anyway?

301
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:19,720
It's like saying, you know,

302
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:21,240
some concepts you came up from homework problems,

303
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:22,840
maybe you can, like the second one,

304
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:24,600
at least apply to the real world,

305
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:28,400
but we could have gone and we could come up

306
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:29,400
with a new efficiency standard,

307
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:31,480
say, you know what, we should maximize

308
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:33,880
like the number of trees,

309
00:11:33,880 --> 00:11:36,380
or the number of copies of Brian's books.

310
00:11:36,380 --> 00:11:37,920
That's what society should be all about.

311
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:39,800
We could go and analyze everything by

312
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:42,520
Kaplanian book maximization efficiency,

313
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:43,340
and then we'd say, like,

314
00:11:43,340 --> 00:11:45,560
how can we squeeze more of his books out of the system?

315
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:46,400
How can we do that?

316
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:48,240
And if that were the main thing we're talking about,

317
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:50,560
then you could make that your standard.

318
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:52,000
There's an unlimited number of standards

319
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,120
that you can adopt.

320
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:56,600
So why are these plausible in any way?

321
00:11:56,600 --> 00:12:00,120
Well, there is the hard question of,

322
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:02,120
is there anyone who really believes

323
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:03,900
that this is the only principle

324
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:05,700
that anyone should care about?

325
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:07,400
So, you can do this for both.

326
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:09,520
So is there anyone who really believes

327
00:12:09,520 --> 00:12:11,520
that everything in the world

328
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:13,800
should be about Pareto efficiency?

329
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:15,480
Right, so, unlikely.

330
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:16,720
How about Calgary sufficiency?

331
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:18,640
There's a couple people I've encountered

332
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:20,520
who at least claim to believe that.

333
00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:22,560
So, Stephen Chabelle at Harvard

334
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:23,960
has given multiple talks

335
00:12:23,960 --> 00:12:25,420
where he actually had time to prepare,

336
00:12:25,420 --> 00:12:27,160
was not enough to cut from arc,

337
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:29,080
where it came down to,

338
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:30,880
there are those of us who favor

339
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:33,640
doing nothing except maximizing counter-efficiency,

340
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:35,320
and then there is the dark side.

341
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:38,480
Everybody who believes anything other than that.

342
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:40,440
It's horrible other people.

343
00:12:40,440 --> 00:12:41,800
All right, so there are a few people

344
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:42,640
who sound like that.

345
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:43,880
If you read early Richard Posner,

346
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:44,720
it sounds like that,

347
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:47,520
although late Richard Posner has,

348
00:12:47,520 --> 00:12:49,080
I think he's totally repudiated it,

349
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:50,520
and now I'm just a pragmatist,

350
00:12:50,520 --> 00:12:53,280
so no, I'm not gonna have any big standard

351
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:54,920
of anything anymore.

352
00:12:54,920 --> 00:12:56,020
I'm not gonna do that.

353
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:58,440
All right, so anyway,

354
00:12:58,440 --> 00:12:59,640
is there anyone who really believes

355
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:00,560
the right action is always the one

356
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:02,200
that is most for Calgary sufficiency?

357
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,280
There is one other person

358
00:13:03,280 --> 00:13:05,120
who sometimes sounds this way,

359
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:06,040
and that is Robin Hanson,

360
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:07,720
my own beloved colleague,

361
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:10,040
who is a wonderful human being in every way,

362
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:13,240
but he has bitten some bullets so grotesque

363
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:17,180
in loyalty to Calgary sufficiency.

364
00:13:17,180 --> 00:13:18,680
So yes, so remember,

365
00:13:18,680 --> 00:13:20,440
this is in no way criticism of Robin.

366
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:22,720
He's just logically deducing the conclusions

367
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:24,080
from the position,

368
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:25,880
but I say, well, I know,

369
00:13:25,880 --> 00:13:27,800
but he once said this,

370
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:29,800
don't judge him, listen.

371
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:31,640
He said, the main problem with the Holocaust

372
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:33,120
is there weren't enough Nazis.

373
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:36,440
And then, oh, of course,

374
00:13:36,440 --> 00:13:37,680
now I know what you're saying.

375
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:40,560
It's like, look, if there were a trillion Nazis,

376
00:13:40,560 --> 00:13:43,120
each of whom enjoyed the spectacle of the Holocaust

377
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:45,280
at like $10,000 each,

378
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:48,080
and the number of Jews is so small in comparison,

379
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:49,640
then the dollar value of the Nazis

380
00:13:49,640 --> 00:13:51,000
are getting to be so great,

381
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,480
it would out overshadow any loss of the Jews,

382
00:13:53,480 --> 00:13:54,760
and so it would fit the standards,

383
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:56,680
so it would be very unfair and biased

384
00:13:56,680 --> 00:13:58,760
to be against those Nazis.

385
00:13:58,760 --> 00:14:01,240
All right, and yes,

386
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:02,760
all right, now, is he pro-Nazi?

387
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:04,360
Of course not.

388
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:05,980
Not in the slightest way.

389
00:14:05,980 --> 00:14:08,400
All he is doing is applying the doctrine

390
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:10,800
and saying, well, that just needs to be applied by it.

391
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:12,000
And you say, look, that would never happen

392
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,400
because there aren't a trillion Nazis.

393
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:15,240
Right, I agree.

394
00:14:15,240 --> 00:14:17,000
I think it's unlikely there are Nazis

395
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
in space out there in mass numbers

396
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,360
going and watching horrible atrocities

397
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:23,320
as paid for view or something like that,

398
00:14:23,320 --> 00:14:26,120
but we can still imagine,

399
00:14:26,120 --> 00:14:27,480
the question of the hypothetical is,

400
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:30,600
if there were, just multiplying the number

401
00:14:30,600 --> 00:14:35,280
of sadistic, horrible people watching it makes it good?

402
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:37,040
Well, that would be applied by the doctrine.

403
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:38,400
And yes, and also I brought up,

404
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:40,640
well, what about, there's a cannibal lector

405
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:42,440
who enjoys eating children,

406
00:14:42,440 --> 00:14:44,160
and there's a bunch of penniless orphans

407
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:46,720
who are never going to be any richer than they are now,

408
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:48,880
so they can't borrow against their future earnings,

409
00:14:48,880 --> 00:14:49,800
and we have an auction,

410
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:51,680
and Hannibal Lecter gets to buy all the children

411
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:52,760
and eat them,

412
00:14:52,760 --> 00:14:54,280
because the orphans are allowed to borrow,

413
00:14:54,280 --> 00:14:55,120
they're allowed to bid,

414
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:56,600
but they've got nothing to bid with.

415
00:14:56,600 --> 00:14:59,240
And then the good outcome is for Hannibal Lecter

416
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:01,160
to get to eat the orphans.

417
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:03,280
And I said, well, we wouldn't want to be biased

418
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:04,240
against Hannibal Lecter.

419
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:06,680
It would be very unfair if we were seen

420
00:15:06,680 --> 00:15:07,920
as having these biases.

421
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:10,560
And he's also made an argument

422
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:11,400
that seems really odd to me,

423
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:12,800
which is people won't trust economists

424
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:14,480
unless we go and bite these bullets.

425
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:18,280
No, they will trust us only

426
00:15:18,280 --> 00:15:20,480
if we refuse to bite these bullets.

427
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:24,400
Well, once we go and throw Hannibal Lecter under the bus,

428
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:26,320
what about all these other people?

429
00:15:26,320 --> 00:15:28,120
Well, we have to consider them case by case

430
00:15:28,120 --> 00:15:30,480
to see whether or not throwing them under the bus

431
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:32,560
will lead people to trust us more or less.

432
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:35,000
It's a verbal question, but anyway.

433
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:36,960
So there are a few people who do bite these bullets,

434
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:39,000
and I will say that when I debated Robin,

435
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:40,160
he gained votes.

436
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:42,960
There's a before and after vote,

437
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:43,960
and I said all this,

438
00:15:43,960 --> 00:15:46,120
and he came and he gained votes compared to me,

439
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:49,920
so there must be something persuasive about this

440
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:51,120
as expressed by him.

441
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:53,760
Was the vote cast before he gave his second speech?

442
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:56,120
It was, yeah, so it was before either talked

443
00:15:56,120 --> 00:15:57,840
and then after we were all done.

444
00:15:57,840 --> 00:16:00,280
So they didn't listen to your performance and then vote?

445
00:16:00,280 --> 00:16:01,120
No, no, no, no.

446
00:16:01,120 --> 00:16:02,520
I'm just talking about the effort.

447
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:03,720
They heard, yes.

448
00:16:03,720 --> 00:16:04,560
Roman effort.

449
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:06,560
Yeah, they heard both,

450
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:08,800
so it was not like the Roman effort, yes.

451
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:12,080
All right, now one reply that many economists love

452
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:15,280
is to say, look, calorie sufficiency is fantastic.

453
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:17,200
We just have one little tweak,

454
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:18,920
and that's to combine it with redistribution

455
00:16:18,920 --> 00:16:22,520
based upon wealth, and then we should do exactly that.

456
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:25,040
So the solution here is we just need to redistribute

457
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:26,200
from the head of electorate to the orphans,

458
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:28,600
and then he won't be able to get the orphans anymore.

459
00:16:28,600 --> 00:16:31,480
Or we just need to go and redistribute

460
00:16:31,480 --> 00:16:33,760
from the Nazis to the victims, and then,

461
00:16:33,760 --> 00:16:35,920
but wait a minute, but there's a trillion of those Nazis,

462
00:16:35,920 --> 00:16:37,760
so even after we equalize the wealth,

463
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:38,840
they still might be able to get their way,

464
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:40,160
so this redistribution doesn't seem like

465
00:16:40,160 --> 00:16:43,120
it's really gonna work very well for this problem, does it?

466
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:44,040
And then you're back to, well,

467
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:46,480
but there aren't a trillion Nazis, which is true,

468
00:16:46,480 --> 00:16:48,920
but seems totally inadequate to the hypothetical.

469
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:52,560
But if there were, just getting more horrible people

470
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:54,640
changes the answer to murder?

471
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:58,000
That doesn't seem like it.

472
00:16:58,000 --> 00:16:58,840
You're biased.

473
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:02,400
Okay, all right, so anyway,

474
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:03,720
so this seems inadequate to me,

475
00:17:03,720 --> 00:17:05,680
so there's lots of other issues

476
00:17:05,680 --> 00:17:06,640
that seem like they're relevant,

477
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:09,680
and again, once you violate calorie sufficiency

478
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:13,200
in one way, why not violate it in lots of ways?

479
00:17:13,200 --> 00:17:15,200
So once you're willing to redistribute

480
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:18,200
based upon, like, waste upon wealth,

481
00:17:18,200 --> 00:17:20,160
then how about considering things like dessert?

482
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:23,320
Like, one person worked for the product and deserves it,

483
00:17:23,320 --> 00:17:24,680
and the other person was a lazy bum

484
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:26,320
and doesn't deserve anything.

485
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:28,440
That's a common view, right?

486
00:17:28,440 --> 00:17:30,640
Certainly, when professors are handing out grades,

487
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:33,400
I know almost no one egalitarian enough to say,

488
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:36,120
I give the A's to the students who need it the most.

489
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:37,800
And then I give C's to good students,

490
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:39,320
because they're gonna be doing well in life anyway,

491
00:17:39,320 --> 00:17:41,200
so they can afford to get a C.

492
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:42,280
I don't know anyone who does this.

493
00:17:42,280 --> 00:17:46,360
Instead, even very philosophically egalitarian professors,

494
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:49,280
they say, well, look, some people understand the material

495
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:50,800
and they deserve a good grade,

496
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:51,640
and other people don't,

497
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:52,800
and they don't deserve a good grade.

498
00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:53,640
And you say, well, but the people

499
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:55,480
that don't understand the material

500
00:17:55,480 --> 00:17:56,880
didn't have the same advantages.

501
00:17:56,880 --> 00:17:58,080
Yeah, blah, blah, blah.

502
00:17:58,080 --> 00:17:59,880
All right, I'm still giving it to the people

503
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:01,680
who understand political philosophy.

504
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:03,520
If you can't explain Rawls accurately,

505
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:07,120
then you deserve to get F's and have a low income.

506
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:10,440
But, well, anyway, that's not my problem.

507
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:12,840
Let the government solve the problem

508
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:14,040
with the low income of the people

509
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:15,400
who got F's in political philosophy.

510
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:18,440
What I do is give A's to the deserving people

511
00:18:18,440 --> 00:18:20,160
who understand Rawls,

512
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:23,920
and F's to the reprobates who didn't do their work.

513
00:18:23,920 --> 00:18:24,760
All right?

514
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:25,600
I did.

515
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:29,240
Yes, and if this has any plausibility to you,

516
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:30,360
that you should not give grades

517
00:18:30,360 --> 00:18:32,560
based upon whether someone needs them,

518
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:34,080
I have had students who come and say,

519
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:36,000
I really need this grade.

520
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:39,440
Unfortunately, my grading system is based upon desert.

521
00:18:39,440 --> 00:18:41,160
So it's based on whether you deserve a grade,

522
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:42,000
not whether you need a grade.

523
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,480
So many people deserve a grade but don't need it,

524
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:46,080
and other people need a grade they don't deserve.

525
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:48,120
So, and when that happens,

526
00:18:48,120 --> 00:18:49,680
normally I don't actually go tell them this.

527
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:50,680
I just say no.

528
00:18:50,680 --> 00:18:53,960
But if they were to philosophically challenge me,

529
00:18:53,960 --> 00:18:55,720
then probably I would just say,

530
00:18:55,720 --> 00:18:57,560
I don't wanna discuss this philosophically.

531
00:18:57,560 --> 00:18:59,960
But if I did, I'd say, well, here's my view.

532
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:02,960
All right, and so desert,

533
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:05,080
and so if you've read Robert Nozick's

534
00:19:05,080 --> 00:19:06,200
Anarchy, State, and Utopia,

535
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:08,800
you may be familiar with the entitlement theory of justice.

536
00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:10,440
Maybe there's an example involving Wilt Chamberlain,

537
00:19:10,440 --> 00:19:11,280
which I won't go into,

538
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:13,360
but you can go and Google Wilt Chamberlain example

539
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:14,760
and find out about it.

540
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:18,720
So anyway, my view is just that efficiency

541
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:20,980
is probably one thing we're thinking about.

542
00:19:20,980 --> 00:19:24,640
So you could be what philosophers will call a pluralist,

543
00:19:24,640 --> 00:19:25,880
says, you know what's good?

544
00:19:25,880 --> 00:19:27,280
Lots of different things.

545
00:19:27,280 --> 00:19:29,360
One of them's counter-insufficiency.

546
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:30,200
It's just one.

547
00:19:30,200 --> 00:19:31,760
There's lots of other things that matter.

548
00:19:31,760 --> 00:19:35,200
So that seems like a more reasonable view.

549
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:37,520
I'll say, why should economists focus so much

550
00:19:37,520 --> 00:19:38,920
on this one thing?

551
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:41,040
And there an answer might be because

552
00:19:41,040 --> 00:19:43,720
economists have special training for distinguishing

553
00:19:43,720 --> 00:19:45,080
deadweight costs from transfers,

554
00:19:45,080 --> 00:19:47,280
which allows us to say something especially useful

555
00:19:47,280 --> 00:19:49,060
about this one thing that matters.

556
00:19:49,060 --> 00:19:50,760
Whereas we don't have any special training

557
00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:52,800
on entitlement or desert,

558
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:56,320
or say, we probably don't even have special training

559
00:19:56,320 --> 00:19:58,920
on how quickly happiness declines

560
00:19:58,920 --> 00:20:01,880
as a function of your wealth.


